    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c3da96c7-b633-5882-ad6e-68b6ed9488b7",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\frac{-3\\sin\\left(49^\\circ\\right)\\cos\\left(49^\\circ\\right)}{\\cos\\left(98^\\circ\\right)}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put $x=49^\\circ$. Recall $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin(2x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{-3\\sin x\\cos x}{\\cos(2x)} &=-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{2\\sin x\\cos x}{\\cos(2x)} \\\\ &=-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\sin(2x)}{\\cos(2x)} \\\\ &=-\\frac{3}{2}\\tan(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is nonzero, and $\\tan(2x)=\\sin(2x)/\\cos(2x)$.\n\nSubstitute $x=49^\\circ$ and double the whole angle: $2x=98^\\circ$. The result is\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{2}\\tan\\left(98^\\circ\\right) .\n$$",
